      "text": "Big Sandy was officially incorporated in 1903, marking its transition from a small settlement to an incorporated town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sandy%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits on the Memphis division of the Louisville and Nashville Railroad, a key rail line that once linked the region to larger markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://genealogytrails.com/tenn/benton/history_towns.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Big Sandy’s origins trace back to a simple water tank and fuel yard that attracted early settlers and businesses.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sandy%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Drivers will notice the town’s core at the crossroads of State Route 69A and State Route 147, a historic junction for local travel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sandy%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Geographically, Big Sandy lies at 36°14′3″N 88°5′9″W, placing it in northwestern Tennessee near the Tennessee River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Sandy%2C_Tennessee"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s name reflects the nearby Big Sandy Creek, a tributary that once powered early mills and shaped the local landscape.",
